A method for controlling a robot to manipulate an unknown object may comprise:

1. acquiring, by one or more sensors, three-dimensional shape information representing the unknown object;
2. generating a geometric model of the unknown object from the acquired shape information;
3. comparing the geometric model with respective reference-shape models of a plurality of registered objects stored in a memory;
4. selecting a registered object based on a similarity between the geometric model and the reference-shape model of the registered object;
5. retrieving a stored manipulation operation associated with the selected registered object, the stored manipulation operation specifying at least one of a robot trajectory, an end-effector pose, a contact location, a gripping configuration, or an applied force;
6. determining a deformation mapping that maps the reference-shape model of the selected registered object onto the geometric model of the unknown object;
7. deforming the stored manipulation operation in accordance with the deformation mapping to generate an adapted manipulation operation for the unknown object; and
8. controlling the robot to manipulate the unknown object according to the adapted manipulation operation.

The deformation mapping may comprise a rigid, affine, articulated, or non-rigid transformation and may be constrained to preserve one or more task-relevant relationships, including approach direction, contact orientation, grasp width, collision clearance, and force direction. During execution, sensor feedback may be used to update the deformation mapping or the adapted manipulation operation in response to a detected difference between an expected object state and a measured object state.
